# Bounded rotation for Mystic process logs.
#
# start_mystic.sh redirects each process's stdout/stderr into logs/*.log with a
# shell redirect, so the descriptor stays open for the life of the process and
# Python's RotatingFileHandler does not apply. `copytruncate` is therefore
# required: the active log is copied and then truncated in place, so the running
# process keeps writing to the same descriptor and no log is deleted or renamed
# out from under it. Because the copy is a separate file that nothing writes to,
# it is safe to gzip it on the same pass rather than deferring a cycle.
#
# Install (as root on mystic-prod):
#   cp /home/mystic/mystic/deploy/logrotate-mystic /etc/logrotate.d/mystic
#   chmod 644 /etc/logrotate.d/mystic
#   logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.d/mystic    # verify only, changes nothing
#   logrotate --force /etc/logrotate.d/mystic    # first rotation
#
# Retention: the 20 MB rule fires about hourly on the busiest logs, so the
# backup count is the retention window. 60 backups keep at least 48 hours of
# every log; maxage bounds the slow logs. Compressed backups measure 0.25-1.6 MB
# each (mystic_portfolio.log is the largest), so the worst case is about
# 20 MB active + 60 x 1.6 MB = ~116 MB per busy log, ~250 MB in total.

/home/mystic/mystic/logs/*.log
/home/mystic/.cursor/debug.log {
    size 20M
    rotate 60
    maxage 7
    compress
    missingok
    notifempty
    copytruncate
    su mystic mystic
}
